                        <forename>Peter George</forename>
                        <surname>Patmore</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1786">
                        <placeName/>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1855"/>
                    <occupation>Author</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Probably P G Patmore, celebrated for his Imitations of celebbrtated
                            authors; and his My Friends and Acquaintances. Associated with
                            Pickersgill's circle - although Pickersgill is not mentioned in his
                            memoirs. His son Coventry Kersey Dichton Patmore was born in 1823 -
                            which sugest that the plural 'Patmores' in 1831 may be Patmore and his
                            wife (Eliza Anne Bowring Robertson 1791-1851). See ODNB. Note also his somewhat pariah status following his
                            involvement as a second in a duel in 1821, at which John Scott was
                            killed. His reputation was forther sullied by his involvement in Hazlitt's 'Liber Amoris' (1823).</p>
                    </note>
